    {
      "id": "2.1",
      "title": "Sort the Mail",
      "story": "A sack of customer notes lands on the bridge. Some praise the bread and some grumble about the queue. \"Sort them,\" says Wren, \"but there are hundreds.\" Kit smiles. \"Then let's teach a program to do it.\"",
      "everyday": {
        "scene": "Your email app files messages into Inbox and Spam without you reading them, and a shop sorts its reviews into happy and unhappy ones.",
        "question": "How can a program sort text into groups it has never read?",
        "answer": "It turns each text into numbers, such as word counts, and uses patterns in those numbers to pick a group. That is text classification."
      },
      "lessons": [
        {
          "id": "bag-of-words",
          "title": "A bag of words",
          "text": [
            "To classify text, first turn it into numbers. The bag-of-words idea counts how many times each word appears and throws away the order. 'Owl chases fox' and 'fox chases owl' give the same bag.",
            "The table shows counts for three short notes. Each note becomes a column of counts over the whole vocabulary. Order is lost, but counts are quick to work with and often good enough for sorting."
          ],
          "terms": [
            {
              "term": "Bag of words",
              "meaning": "A text described only by how often each word appears."
            },
            {
              "term": "Vocabulary",
              "meaning": "All the different words across the notes."
            }
          ],
