        {
          "id": "tf-idf",
          "title": "Rare words matter more",
          "text": [
            "Counting treats every word alike, but a rare word tells you more. A common word like 'and' says little. A scheme called tf-idf multiplies two things: how often a word appears in this note, and how rare the word is across all the notes.",
            {
              "text": "In one common version, tf is a word's count divided by the note's length, and idf is log base 10 of (notes divided by notes containing the word). In note 1, 'fresh' scores about 0.07. In note 2, 'stale' scores about 0.12.",
              "verify": false
            }
          ],
          "table": {
            "head": [
              "Word",
              "Notes containing it (of 3)",
              "idf (log base 10)"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "and",
                "2",
                "0.18"
              ],
              [
                "fresh",
                "2",
                "0.18"
              ],
              [
                "stale",
                "1",
                "0.48"
              ]
            ]
          },
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Wikipedia: tf-idf",
              "source": "Wikipedia (linked, not copied)",
              "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tf%E2%80%93idf"
            },
            {
              "title": "scikit-learn user guide: Feature extraction (text)",
              "source": "scikit-learn user guide (linked, not copied)",
              "url": "https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/feature_extraction.html"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "weighted-sum",
          "title": "Scoring and checking",
          "text": [
            "A simple classifier gives each word a weight, adds up the weights of the words in the text, and picks a label by the sign. A positive total means praise, a negative total means complaint. Real systems learn the weights from labelled examples.",
            "Then you check it on texts it has not seen. In our small test of 10 reviews, the weights got 8 right, which is 80 percent accuracy. One miss was a tie, where praise and complaint cancelled to 0. The other used only words with no weight."
          ],
          "deeper": {
            "title": "Where the weights come from",
            "text": [
              {
                "text": "Methods like naive Bayes can learn from labelled examples to classify text. Here the weights are simply given.",
                "verify": false
              }
            ]
          },
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Wikipedia: Document classification",
              "source": "Wikipedia (linked, not copied)",
              "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Document_classification"
            },
